/* Brand extension color tokens — LIGHT (DESIGN_IMPLEMENTATION.md §3.2).
 * Consume as: background-color: var(--md-sys-color-success-container);
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
 *  M3's role set has `error` but no `success` and no `warning`, and V2 needs both
 *  (the "Risco: Baixo" badge and the lifeguard alert bar). `_colors-*.uss` is
 *  GENERATED from tools/design_system/brand-theme.json and must not be hand-edited,
 *  so the extensions get their own hand-maintained pair, following the
 *  _shape / _state / _motion precedent. They keep the `--md-sys-color-*` prefix on
 *  purpose: components should not care whether a color role is stock M3 or ours.
 *
 * THREE THINGS MOVE IN LOCKSTEP WITH THIS FILE OR THE SUITE GOES RED
 *  1. _brand-dark.uss must define the IDENTICAL variable set.
 *  2. Theme-Light.tss / Theme-Dark.tss each @import their side (parity-tested).
 *  3. TokenDisciplineTests.TokenFiles(theme) lists these files explicitly.
 *
 *  token                    | value   | provenance
 *  -------------------------|---------|---------------------------------------------
 *  success                  | #34C759 | brand — V2 "Risco: Baixo" badge (A.1)
 *  on-success               | #052E16 | green tone 10 (6.7:1 on success)
 *  success-container        | #DCFCE7 | green tone 95
 *  on-success-container     | #14532D | green tone 30 (8.3:1 on the container)
 *  warning                  | #FEDF89 | brand — V2 alert-bar border (A.1)
 *  on-warning               | #152336 | brand navy (12.2:1) — the warning hue IS the
 *                           |         | brand amber hue, so it pairs with navy like
 *                           |         | `on-secondary` does, not with a brown
 *  warning-container        | #FFF7E6 | brand — V2 alert-bar fill (A.1)
 *  on-warning-container     | #152336 | brand navy (14.9:1)
 *
 *  Green is the one hue in the system that is neither navy nor amber; it is
 *  mandated by the design (#34C759) and so carries its own tonal on-colors.
 *
 *  ---- Camera-overlay family (Slice 4, the AR HUD — §8.4) -------------------
 *  Surfaces that float over the LIVE CAMERA rather than over a themed page, so
 *  they are the one group whose values are identical in light and dark: the
 *  backdrop is whatever the lens is pointing at, and it is never the theme.
 *  Using inverse-surface for the info card would have been the obvious M3 role
 *  (light = the exact brand value) but it FLIPS to a light grey in dark, which
 *  would render the card white-on-camera; hence an explicit pair here.
 *
 *  THE FIVE HAIRLINE/FILL VALUES ARE PRE-BLENDED COMPOSITES, NOT ALPHAS.
 *  Unity renders in LINEAR color space (ProjectSettings m_ActiveColorSpace: 1):
 *  a USS color is converted to linear, blended, and converted back. Figma blends
 *  directly in sRGB. So a translucent value transcribed from Figma composites
 *  differently in Unity — always landing too LIGHT. Measured: white at 0.15 over
 *  the info card rendered (108,109,110) where V2 shows (42,48,55). The per-channel
 *  alphas that would undo that diverge (0.022 / 0.026 / 0.032 for that one case),
 *  so no single alpha reproduces Figma. Where the backdrop is OPAQUE AND KNOWN —
 *  which every one of these is, the AR card and the pill both being opaque — the
 *  exact fix is to author the composite itself. That is what these are.
 *
 *  CONSEQUENCE, AND THE ONE THING THAT CAN SILENTLY ROT: each value is bonded to
 *  ONE backdrop. Change camera-surface or camera-surface-dim and every composite
 *  below has to be recomputed against the new backdrop — they will not follow on
 *  their own the way an alpha would. That coupling is why they are named after
 *  the surface they are drawn ON, not after how translucent they look.
 *
 *  token                     | value   | = Figma paint      over            | site
 *  --------------------------|---------|--------------------|--------------|-----------------------
 *  camera-surface            | #3A3F45 | opaque             | —            | back circle + beach pill
 *  camera-surface-dim        | #050B14 | opaque             | —            | species info-card fill
 *  on-camera-surface         | #FFFFFF | opaque             | —            | every glyph and label
 *  camera-outline            | #2A3037 | white @ 0.15       | surface-dim  | info-card hairline
 *  camera-outline-dim        | #54585D | white @ 0.13       | surface      | control hairlines (pill, back)
 *  camera-divider            | #262B33 | white @ 0.13       | surface-dim  | the card's internal rule
 *  camera-fill               | #1E232C | white @ 0.10       | surface-dim  | close-button disc fill
 *  camera-fill-outline       | #40444C | white @ 0.15       | camera-fill  | close-button disc hairline
 *
 *  NAMING WART, KEPT ON PURPOSE: post-pre-blend `camera-outline-dim` is LIGHTER
 *  than `camera-outline`, because "dim" named the smaller alpha (0.13 vs 0.15)
 *  and the two sit on different backdrops. The names are kept so the app-side
 *  MergulhoScreen.uss that consumes them does not have to move in the same
 *  change; rename both to say where they are drawn once that file is free.
 *
 *  ---- Muted ink on the inverse surface (bottom navigation bar) --------------
 *  Appendix A.1 maps the inactive tab's "white at 50%" to inverse-on-surface at
 *  50%. Same linear-space problem, same fix: the bar is opaque inverse-surface,
 *  so the composite is authored directly. Authored as an `opacity` it rendered
 *  (183,184,185) in light against V2's (127,130,136) — the inactive tabs read
 *  almost as bright as the active one. Unlike the camera family this one DOES
 *  differ per theme, because both the ink and the bar flip.
 *
 *  token                     | light   | dark    | = inverse-on-surface @ 0.5 over inverse-surface
 *  --------------------------|---------|---------|------------------------------------------------
 *  inverse-on-surface-muted  | #7F8288 | #828891 | inactive nav-bar icon + label
 */
:root {
    --md-sys-color-success: #34C759;
    --md-sys-color-on-success: #052E16;
    --md-sys-color-success-container: #DCFCE7;
    --md-sys-color-on-success-container: #14532D;

    --md-sys-color-warning: #FEDF89;
    --md-sys-color-on-warning: #152336;
    --md-sys-color-warning-container: #FFF7E6;
    --md-sys-color-on-warning-container: #152336;

    /* Camera overlays (AR HUD) — see the header table. Same in both themes.
     * PRE-BLENDED composites, not alphas: each is bonded to the backdrop named
     * in that table. Recompute them if camera-surface(-dim) ever moves. */
    --md-sys-color-camera-surface: #3A3F45;
    --md-sys-color-camera-surface-dim: #050B14;
    --md-sys-color-on-camera-surface: #FFFFFF;
    --md-sys-color-camera-outline: #2A3037;
    --md-sys-color-camera-outline-dim: #54585D;
    --md-sys-color-camera-divider: #262B33;
    --md-sys-color-camera-fill: #1E232C;
    --md-sys-color-camera-fill-outline: #40444C;

    /* Inactive bottom-nav ink — see the header table. Pre-blended, per theme. */
    --md-sys-color-inverse-on-surface-muted: #7F8288;
}
